  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1207 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAn upper-level shortwave trough will move across the southern and \ncentral Plains on Saturday.  This feature should weaken the ridge \nand allow low (<30%) rain chances for Saturday and Saturday night, \nmainly across the Big Country. Otherwise, warm and humid conditions \nwith southeasterly surface flow will continue through Monday.  This \nwill result in persistent high temperatures in the mid 90s with some \nlow chances (<10%) for brief light showers each afternoon.\n\nA long-awaited pattern shift appears more likely for the middle of \nnext week starting Tuesday.  Unfortunately, this comes with a very \nmurky and uncertain synoptic setup for the southern Plains. Starting \nwith the more confident details, models are in good agreement on an \nupper-level trough moving out of the Pacific Northwest and into \nTexas Tuesday and Wednesday. At the surface a fairly strong Pacific \ncold front will push southeastward into north Texas Wednesday \nthrough Thursday. This could potentially bring more seasonal \ntemperatures into west central Texas for mid-week. The ensemble \nspread of temperatures ranges from highs in the upper 70s to around \n90 degrees with a mean in the low 80s. The forecast will hinge \nsignificantly on the strength of this front and how far south this \nfeature makes it into Texas. For now, high temperatures were kept in \nthe low to mid 90s for Tuesday and in the mid 80s for Wednesday and \nThursday. The other uncertainty is rainfall chances and timing, \nwhich will depend heavily on the path of Hurricane Polo as it moves \nclose to Baja California in Mexico. Global models have come into \nmuch better agreement on bringing Polo into northwest Mexico on \nTuesday.  If this scenario were to play out Polo's remnants would \nmerge with the aforementioned trough and cold front which would \nincrease chances for heavy rain.  There is quite a bit of \nuncertainty on where heavy rain could occur and would depend heavily \non just how these three features (trough, front and tropical \nremnants) interact. For now, PoPs were kept at low to medium (25-\n50%) for Tuesday through Thursday.",
